{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789427398,"nonce":"nwuKP8TQLsCX9AJqQeuXEK/KKqA=","title":"Collector on cost a quarter of alloc-heavy kernels without collecting","message":"With the collector enabled, kernels that never collect ran slower: op_overload 65 to 80 ms, any_cast and any_field 6.1 to 7.9 ms, op_overload_ref 6.6 to 8.0 ms (zypy, 5 runs, ZYNTAX_DISABLE_GC=1 as the control on the same build). ZYNTAX_TRACE_GC=1 shows zero collections on all of them.\n\nThe cost was per release: `zyntax_free` called `note_thread`, which read the collector's thread-local `Local` (a 40-byte copy through a second thread-local lookup, a call on macOS) and the owner generation on every free, on top of the free list's own thread-local lookup. Allocation paid a third lookup for the swept list when the free list was empty.\n\nFix: the pool's per-thread state is one struct (free lists, swept lists, slab cursors, and the collector's owner mark for the thread), so a release reaches the mark and the list through one lookup; the mark is the generation the thread was last confirmed the owner for, so the check is two loads and a compare. The allocation fast path is one lookup too, with collection and refill in an out-of-line slow path.\n\nAfter: op_overload 64.6 ms (off: 62.2), any_cast 6.46 (6.2), any_field 6.46 (6.2), op_overload_ref 6.5 (6.25), binary_trees 295 (off: 273 to 285, the rest is collection work); branchy unchanged at 90.","files":null}]}